<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="section-integrals-usub" >
<title>Integration using Substitution</title>

<introduction>
	<p>Standards
		<ul>
			<li>Integrate using substitution</li>
		</ul>
	</p>

	<p>We learned that we can use the scalar multiple and sum properties of derivatives to calculate anti-derivatives. However, neither the product rule of derivatives nor anything else produces a product rule for integrals. Now we will consider how we can apply the chain rule of derivatives to find more anti-derivatives.</p>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-discover-usub">
	<title>Using the Chain Rule backwards</title>

<activity xml:id="activity-discover-usub">
	<title>Reversing the Chain Rule</title>
	<introduction><p>The goal of this activity is to recognize how we can use the Chain Rule to find anti-derivatives.</p></introduction>
	<task><p> What is the derivative of <m>\sin u</m>?</p></task>
	<task><p> What is <m>\int \cos u \; du </m>?</p></task>
	<task><p> What is the derivative of <m>\sin (x^2)</m>?</p></task>
	<task><p> What is <m> \int \cos(x^2)2x \; dx </m></p></task>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="activity-find-u">
	<title>Identifying Substitutions</title>
	<introduction><p>The goal of this activity is to learn to identify substitutions for finding anti-derivatives.</p></introduction>
	<task><p> For each of the following integrals determine first if a substitution could be used. Second identify the substitution <m>u</m>.</p>
	<p><ol>
	<li><m>\int \cos(x^2+5x+3)(2x+5) \; dx</m></li>
	<li><m>\int e^{\sin x} \cos x \; dx</m></li>
	<li><m>\int \sin^{76} \theta \cos\theta \; d\theta</m></li>
	<li><m>\int (x^3-x)^{14}3x^2 \; dx</m></li>
	</ol></p>
	</task>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-usub-examples">
	<title>Examples of integral substitution</title>


<example xml:id="example-usub-basic">
	<title>Using substitution</title>
	<statement><p><m>\int 2e^{2x+1} \; dx</m></p></statement>
	<solution><p><md>
		<mrow>\int 2e^{2x+1} \; dx \amp = \amp \begin{array}{rcl} u \amp = \amp 2x+1 \\ du \amp = \amp 2 \; dx \end{array}</mrow>
		<mrow>\int e^u \; du \amp = \amp e^u+C</mrow>
		<mrow> \amp = \amp e^{2x+1}+C.</mrow>
	</md></p></solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-usub-scalar">
	<title>Substitutions for scalars</title>
	<statement><p><m>\int e^{5x} \; dx</m></p></statement>
	<solution><p><md>
		<mrow>\int e^{5x} \; dx \amp = \amp \begin{array}{rcl} u \amp = \amp 5x \\ du \amp = \amp 5 \; dx \end{array}</mrow>
		<mrow>\frac{1}{5}\int e^{5x} 5 \; dx \amp =</mrow>
		<mrow>\frac{1}{5}\int e^u \; du \amp = \amp \frac{1}{5}e^u+C</mrow>
		<mrow> \amp = \amp \frac{1}{5}e^{5x}+C.</mrow>
	</md></p></solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-usub-definite">
	<title>Substitutions for Definite Integrals</title>
	<statement><p><m>\int_1^{\sqrt{2}} \sin(\pi x^2)2\pi x \; dx</m></p></statement>
	<solution><p><md>
		<mrow>\int_0^{1/\sqrt{2}} \sin(\pi x^2)2\pi x \; dx \amp = \amp \begin{array}{rcl} u \amp = \amp \pi x^2 \\ du \amp = \amp 2\pi x \; dx \end{array}</mrow>
		<mrow>\int_0^{\pi/2} \sin(u) \; du \amp = \amp \left. -\cos(u) \right|_0^{\pi/2} </mrow>
		<mrow> \amp = \amp -\cos(\pi/2) - [-\cos(0)]</mrow>
		<mrow> \amp = \amp 1</mrow>
	</md></p></solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-usub-stewart">
	<title>Multipart Substitutions</title>
	<statement><p><m>\int (x-1)\sqrt{x+1} \; dx</m></p></statement>
	<solution><p><md>
		<mrow>\int (x-1)\sqrt{x+1} \; dx \amp = \amp \begin{array}{rcl} u \amp = \amp x+1 \\ du \amp = \amp dx \\ x \amp = \amp u-1 \end{array}</mrow>
		<mrow>\int (u-1-1)\sqrt{u} \; du \amp =</mrow>
		<mrow>\int (u-2)\sqrt{u} \; du \amp = </mrow>
		<mrow>\int u^{3/2}-2u^{1/2} \; du \amp = \amp \frac{2}{5}u^{5/2}-\frac{4}{3}u^{3/2}+C</mrow>
		<mrow> \amp = \amp \frac{2}{5}(x+1)^{5/2}-\frac{4}{3}(x+1)^{3/2}+C.</mrow>
	</md></p></solution>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>